import int.Int

/// Protocol for producing an `Int` hash that respects structural equality.
///
/// `Hashable.hash(value)` returns an Int such that
/// `Equatable.equal(a, b)` implies `Hashable.hash(a) == Hashable.hash(b)`.
/// `Map` and `Set` find a key by it. The hash is not cryptographic and may
/// change between runs of a program, so use it only inside one run.
///
/// The primitive types `Int`, `Float`, `Bool` and `String` implement
/// `Hashable`, and so does `Unit`, whose one value always hashes the same.
/// To implement it for a custom type:
///
/// ```nomi
/// pub struct Point
///     x: Int
///     y: Int
/// end
///
/// impl Hashable for Point
///     fn hash(p: Point) -> Int
///         Hashable.hash(p.x) + Hashable.hash(p.y) * 31
///     end
/// end
/// ```
///
/// `derive Hashable` writes no function: a value hashes by its shape, as a
/// `Map` key of its type does, each part through its own impl. Which Int it
/// gives is not specified. Equal values hash alike: a type with a written
/// `Equatable` and a derived `Hashable` hashes all its values alike.
pub interface Hashable
    fn hash(value: self) -> Int
end
